        <body>
            <div xml:id="d190">

                <pb facs="0166" n="156" sameAs="frus:frus1949v08/pg156"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/3–149: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Stuart</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1949-03-01">March 1,
                        1949</date>.<lb/>[Received March 2—1:21 a. m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>501. <hi rendition="#italic">Yi Shih Pao</hi> March 1 carries statement on
                    current situation by 50 Nanking professors headed by Liu Pu-tung, prominent
                    legislator, and Ni Ching-yuan, Dean of College of Liberal Arts, Nanking.
                    Statement makes following points: <list>
                        <label>1.</label>
                        <item>Since people all demand peace, they are dissatisfied with Government
                            leaders who are inconsistent and waver between peace and war.</item>
                        <label>2.</label>
                        <item>To demonstrate peace sincerity both sides must stop war preparation.
                            Today only small number of privileged people such as warlords and
                            bureaucrats do not want peace.</item>
                        <label>3.</label>
                        <item>China needs thorough social reform and the evil influences of
                            feudalism, imperialism and bureaucratic capitalism must be
                            uprooted.</item>
                        <label>4.</label>
                        <item>Interference by any friendly nation in China’s domestic affairs or
                            proffer of military aid to prolong China’s civil war should be
                            resolutely opposed.</item>
                    </list>
                </p>
                <p>Sent Department; repeated AmEmb Canton 126.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Stuart</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
